<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tartu</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Dorpat, Yuryev</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tartu is the second-largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonia">Estonia</a>, on the Emajõgi river. An Estonian hillfort there was taken by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yaroslav-the-wise">Yaroslav the Wise</a> around 1030 and named Yuryev, and in 1224 the German crusaders captured it, making it the seat of the Bishopric of Dorpat. The university founded by Gustav II Adolf in 1632 made it Estonia's intellectual centre, and the Treaty of Tartu of 1920 recognised Estonian independence.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tartu.jpg" alt="Tartu" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Boreaallane · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tartu_(2019).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Tartu lies about 186 kilometres southeast of Tallinn on the Emajõgi river, which connects Lake Võrtsjärv with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-peipus">Lake Peipus</a>. Archaeological evidence of permanent settlement on the site dates to as early as the 5th century CE. By the 7th century local inhabitants had built a wooden fortification on the east side of Toome Hill, and around the 9th and 10th centuries the settlement became an inland trading centre. The name is thought to derive from the Estonian word for aurochs, tarvas.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>From the 13th century until the end of the 19th, the town was known in most of the world by variants of Dorpat. In 1262 the army of Prince Dmitri of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pereslavl-zalessky">Pereslavl</a> captured and destroyed it but could not take the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>'s fortress on Toome Hill. In the 1280s Dorpat joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanseatic-league">Hanseatic League</a>. In 1558 Tsar Ivan the Terrible began the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonian-war">Livonian War</a> by besieging it, and the local bishop was imprisoned in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, which ended local self-government. The city then passed between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>, which held it for good from 1625.</p><p>In 1708 Russian forces blew up the remaining fortifications and houses and deported the citizens to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, and with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-nystad">Treaty of Nystad</a> in 1721 the city became part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>. The Great Fire of 1775 destroyed most of the buildings in the centre, and the town hall was built between 1782 and 1789. In the second half of the 19th century Tartu was the cultural centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonians">Estonians</a>: it hosted the first song festival in 1869, the founding of the Vanemuine theatre in 1870 and of the Society of Estonian Literati in 1872. Much of the city was destroyed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, and under Soviet rule the population almost doubled from 57,000 to over 100,000 between 1944 and 1991.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Tartu is often called the intellectual capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonia">Estonia</a>. It is the seat of the Supreme Court of Estonia, the Estonian National Museum, whose new main building opened in 2016, and the Vanemuine theatre, the oldest Estonian-language theatre, and it is the birthplace of the Estonian Song Festivals. After independence was regained in 1991 the old town was renovated and St. John's Church, in ruins since the war, was restored.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tartu” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its Baltic empire reached Livonia, Ingria and Estonia, where Gustav II Adolf founded the University of Dorpat (<b>Tartu</b>) in 1632.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-sweden">Kingdom of Sweden · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The town also sent delegates to the Livonian diets, where the Order, the bishops, the vassal nobility and the towns met, and to Hanseatic assemblies, and Reval, Riga and <b>Dorpat</b> formed the leading Livonian group within the Hanse.</blockquote><a href="/empire/reval">Reval · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>In Estonia and Livonia, Swedish rule is associated with the founding of the University of Dorpat (<b>Tartu</b>) in 1632 and with educational and legal reforms whose institutions survived under later Russian governance, while Swedish Pomerania remained in Swedish hands until 1815.</blockquote><a href="/empire/swedish-empire">Swedish Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Livonian Brothers of the Sword, an order of German crusading knights, took <b>Tartu</b>, or Dorpat, the last major centre of Estonian resistance on the mainland.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-15">15 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The alliance joined the Pereyaslavl, Yaroslavl, <b>Yuryev</b>, Nizhny Novgorod and Murom principalities.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-2">2 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>King Gustavus Adolphus of Sweden, then campaigning in Germany, signed the foundation act of a university in <b>Dorpat</b> (Tartu), months before his death at Lützen.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-30">30 June in history</a></li></ul></section>
